<h1><span lang="EN-GB">New Point - Non-Interactive</span></h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-US">This command is accessible from</span> <i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Datuming &gt; Non-Interactive &gt;</b> <i style="font-style:normal; font-weight:bold;">Point</i></span></i><span lang="EN-US">.</span></p>
<p class="description"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> New Point command creates new datum point.</p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image112.gif" width="433px" height="369px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span lang="EN-GB">C</span>ommon parts of the dialog:</p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Name</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– name for the point. The default name is ‘Point N’, where N is the number of the subsequent newly created datum point.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><b style="font-weight:bold;"><span lang="EN-GB">Result</span>: When all necessary entities are selected from the lists, the result, which contains the co-ordinates of the point, is displayed:</b></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image115.gif" width="433px" height="121px" border="0" class="img_whs5" alt=""></span></p>
<p>&#160;</p>
<h2><span lang="EN-GB">Methods</span></h2>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Co-ordinates</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image028.jpg" width="391px" height="128px" border="0" class="img_whs6" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>X, Y, Z</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– co-ordinates of the point.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Line and Plane</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image030.jpg" width="386px" height="90px" border="0" class="img_whs7" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">The point is defined as the intersection of the plane and the line.</span> <span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>They should not have to be parallel</b></span><span lang="EN-GB">. In case of cylinder, its axis is used as line.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Average</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="using_datums_files/image032.gif" width="385px" height="125px" border="0" class="img_whs8" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>-&gt; button</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select datum points, spheres, and circles from the left hand list and press the button to add them to the right hand list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>&lt;- button</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select datum points, spheres, and circles from the right hand list and press the button to remove them from this list. Then the removed entities are automatically placed in the left hand list.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB">All entities from the right hand list are used for creation of the point. Its co-ordinates are average of the co-ordinates of the selected entities. In case of spheres and circles, their centres are used.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Projection</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/Point_-_Projection.GIF" width="391px" height="93px" border="0" class="img_whs9" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Entity</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane/Line</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">– select an existing datum plane or line from the list.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US" style="font-size:10.0pt; font-family:Arial;"><font size="2" style="font-size:10pt;">The point is defined as the projection of a point onto the plane or the line. In case of spheres and circles, their centres are used as points.</font></span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Three Planes</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image036.jpg" width="389px" height="129px" border="0" class="img_whs10" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane 3</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum plane from the list.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">The point is defined as the intersection of the three planes.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Plane 1, Plane 2, and Plane 3 must be three different planes and should not have to be parallel to each other.</b></span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Line</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image038.jpg" width="389px" height="124px" border="0" class="img_whs11" alt=""></span></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>First point</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– the point is defined from the beginning of the line.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Middle point</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– the point is defined from the middle of the line.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Last point</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– the point is defined from the end of the line.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB">In case of cylinder, its axis is used as line.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Sphere/Circle</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="using_datums_files/image113.gif" width="385px" height="91px" border="0" class="img_whs12" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>From list</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– creates a point from the centre of the sphere or circle selected from the list.</span></p>
<ul>
<li class="kadov-p-Cbullet">
<p class="bullet"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Sphere/Circle</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing sphere or circle from the list.</span></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>From selection</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– creates multiple points from the centres of all visible and selected spheres and circles.</span></p>
<p class="sub-head"><span lang="EN-GB">Line and Line</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="using_datums_files/image114.gif" width="386px" height="123px" border="0" class="img_whs13" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Line 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">– select an existing datum line or cylinder from the list.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">The shortest distance between the lines is automatically calculated and displayed.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">The point is defined as the intersection of the two lines. They should not have to be parallel. In case of cylinders, their axes are used as lines.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">If the lines have no intersection but are not parallel, they are called skew or agonic lines. In this case the point is defined as the middle of the transversal, which is the line connecting the closest points between the two lines.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB"><img src="Using_Datums_files/image044.gif" width="326px" height="249px" border="0" class="img_whs14" alt=""></span><span lang="EN-GB">&#160;</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">This method is useful when you want to find the cross-point of two captured lines, which are not absolutely co-planar.</span></p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify; margin-left:0px;">&#160;</p>
